From: Swift Linux <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not configure the test suite when cross compiling

tests/meson.build decides whether to build the suite by asking the build
machine's python whether it can import gi and dbusmock. That question is
about the wrong machine: the tests import the target's AccountsService
typelib, LD_PRELOAD a libmocklibc built for the target, and load
libaccountsservice out of the build tree, so they can only run where the
build output runs.

While the answer was no the block was skipped and nothing noticed. It
became yes here as a side effect of an unrelated change - host PyGObject
was added so blueprint-compiler could resolve GTK widget names - and
configuring then failed in the first thing the block does:

    tests/meson.build:49:12: ERROR: Command
    `.../tests/unittest_inspector.py .../tests/test-libaccountsservice.py`
    failed with status 1.

which is unittest_inspector.py importing the test module to enumerate its
cases, and failing on the target-only imports inside it.

Gate the block on a native build as well, so the suite is configured when
it could actually be run and left alone otherwise.

--- a/tests/meson.build
+++ b/tests/meson.build
@@ -15,7 +15,11 @@
 envs.prepend('LD_LIBRARY_PATH', accountsservice_build_dir)
 
 python3_test_modules_found = false
-if get_option('introspection')
+# The suite imports the target's AccountsService typelib and LD_PRELOADs a
+# target libmocklibc, so it can only run where the build output runs. Asking
+# the build machine's python whether it can import gi says nothing about
+# that, and answering yes is what drags unittest_inspector.py in.
+if get_option('introspection') and not meson.is_cross_build()
     python3 = find_program('python3') # No meson without it!
     python3_test_modules_found = true
     python3_test_modules = [
